1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,150
A sign in the college lift
says: maximum eight persons.

2
00:00:04,550 --> 00:00:06,025
Eight people step in.

3
00:00:06,025 --> 00:00:06,925
Fine.

4
00:00:06,562 --> 00:00:09,321
A ninth squeezes in, and the alarm beeps.

5
00:00:09,771 --> 00:00:11,505
Aarav saw this happen this morning.

6
00:00:11,855 --> 00:00:12,912
Eight is fine.

7
00:00:12,912 --> 00:00:13,876
Nine is not.

8
00:00:13,876 --> 00:00:15,897
But the sign never says equals.

9
00:00:15,897 --> 00:00:17,927
So what kind of maths is this?

10
00:00:18,427 --> 00:00:20,152
It is called an inequality.

11
00:00:20,152 --> 00:00:21,932
And it opens a new chapter.

12
00:00:22,582 --> 00:00:24,537
First, think of a balance scale.

13
00:00:24,887 --> 00:00:28,101
Same weight on both
pans, and it stays level.

14
00:00:28,101 --> 00:00:31,582
That is an equation, like
x plus two equals five.

15
00:00:31,982 --> 00:00:34,665
One pan heavier, and the scale tips.

16
00:00:34,665 --> 00:00:36,169
One side is bigger.

17
00:00:36,169 --> 00:00:37,829
That is an inequality.

18
00:00:38,229 --> 00:00:39,793
NCERT says it like this.

19
00:00:39,793 --> 00:00:43,975
Two numbers, or two expressions,
joined by one of four symbols, form an

20
00:00:43,975 --> 00:00:44,875
inequality.

21
00:00:45,393 --> 00:00:46,938
Here are the four symbols.

22
00:00:47,288 --> 00:00:48,188
Less than.

23
00:00:47,972 --> 00:00:49,350
Three is less than five.

24
00:00:49,750 --> 00:00:50,650
Greater than.

25
00:00:50,626 --> 00:00:52,260
Seven is greater than five.

26
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:54,237
Less than or equal to.

27
00:00:54,237 --> 00:00:57,535
The little line under it
means equal is allowed too.

28
00:00:57,935 --> 00:00:59,779
Greater than or equal to.

29
00:00:59,779 --> 00:01:01,878
Bigger, or exactly the same.

30
00:01:02,278 --> 00:01:05,929
To remember: the open mouth
always faces the bigger number.

31
00:01:06,579 --> 00:01:07,904
Back to the lift.

32
00:01:07,904 --> 00:01:10,455
Let p be the number of people inside.

33
00:01:10,855 --> 00:01:13,000
Maximum eight means eight or less.

34
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,406
So p is less than or equal to eight.

35
00:01:15,806 --> 00:01:17,158
Watch the test run.

36
00:01:17,158 --> 00:01:20,058
Zero, one, two, all the way up to eight.

37
00:01:20,058 --> 00:01:21,213
True every time.

38
00:01:21,613 --> 00:01:22,513
Nine?

39
00:01:22,138 --> 00:01:23,038
False.

40
00:01:22,709 --> 00:01:23,609
Ten?

41
00:01:23,187 --> 00:01:24,096
False again.

42
00:01:24,746 --> 00:01:27,579
The values that make it
true are its solutions.

43
00:01:27,579 --> 00:01:29,753
Together they form the solution set.

44
00:01:30,153 --> 00:01:31,748
Now remove the little line.

45
00:01:31,748 --> 00:01:33,956
The rule becomes p is less than eight.

46
00:01:34,356 --> 00:01:35,380
Look at eight.

47
00:01:35,380 --> 00:01:36,450
It turns false.

48
00:01:36,450 --> 00:01:38,825
One tiny line, one whole answer gone.

49
00:01:39,475 --> 00:01:40,535
Side by side.

50
00:01:40,535 --> 00:01:41,998
Less than is strict.

51
00:01:41,998 --> 00:01:45,894
The end number is not allowed,
so we draw an empty circle.

52
00:01:46,294 --> 00:01:48,412
Less than or equal to is slack.

53
00:01:48,412 --> 00:01:51,951
The end number is allowed,
so the circle is filled in.

54
00:01:52,551 --> 00:01:53,451
Your turn.

55
00:01:53,287 --> 00:01:56,761
A ride says: at least one hundred
and twenty centimetres tall.

56
00:01:56,761 --> 00:01:57,927
Strict, or slack?

57
00:01:57,927 --> 00:01:59,009
Pause and decide.

58
00:02:02,209 --> 00:02:03,109
Slack!

59
00:02:02,791 --> 00:02:06,424
At least means exactly one
hundred and twenty is fine too.

60
00:02:07,074 --> 00:02:08,152
Remember this.

61
00:02:08,152 --> 00:02:09,643
An equation balances.

62
00:02:09,643 --> 00:02:14,256
An inequality tips, and every value
that keeps it true is a solution.

63
00:02:14,956 --> 00:02:16,585
NCERT gives each kind a name.

64
00:02:16,935 --> 00:02:19,711
Three less than five has only numbers.

65
00:02:19,711 --> 00:02:21,417
A numerical inequality.

66
00:02:21,817 --> 00:02:25,413
x less than five has a
letter for an unknown number.

67
00:02:25,413 --> 00:02:26,929
A literal inequality.

68
00:02:27,329 --> 00:02:31,586
Three less than x less than five
traps x between two numbers.

69
00:02:31,586 --> 00:02:33,101
A double inequality.

70
00:02:33,501 --> 00:02:38,831
And when x is never squared, like a x
plus b less than zero, it is a linear

71
00:02:38,831 --> 00:02:39,734
inequality.

72
00:02:39,734 --> 00:02:42,473
Linear means straight line, no powers.

73
00:02:43,223 --> 00:02:44,809
Now the biggest difference.

74
00:02:45,209 --> 00:02:48,820
x plus two equals five
has exactly one answer.

75
00:02:48,820 --> 00:02:49,965
x is three.

76
00:02:50,365 --> 00:02:55,386
But x plus two less than five is true
for two, one, zero, minus a hundred,

77
00:02:55,386 --> 00:02:56,850
even two point nine.

78
00:02:57,450 --> 00:03:00,021
So how many real numbers make it true?

79
00:03:00,021 --> 00:03:01,223
Pause and think.

80
00:03:04,423 --> 00:03:05,323
Endless!

81
00:03:05,110 --> 00:03:07,693
Every real number less than three works.

82
00:03:07,693 --> 00:03:11,243
But if x must be a natural
number, only one and two.

83
00:03:11,893 --> 00:03:14,065
Now, a worked example from the NCERT book.

84
00:03:14,415 --> 00:03:15,982
Ravi has two hundred rupees.

85
00:03:15,982 --> 00:03:17,839
Rice costs thirty rupees a packet.

86
00:03:17,839 --> 00:03:19,455
How many packets can he buy?

87
00:03:19,955 --> 00:03:20,855
Step one.

88
00:03:20,614 --> 00:03:22,397
Let x be the number of packets.

89
00:03:22,397 --> 00:03:23,882
They cost thirty x rupees.

90
00:03:24,632 --> 00:03:25,532
Step two.

91
00:03:25,404 --> 00:03:29,874
Two hundred is not a multiple of
thirty, so he can never spend it all.

92
00:03:29,874 --> 00:03:32,104
Thirty x is less than two hundred.

93
00:03:32,854 --> 00:03:33,754
Step three.

94
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:35,922
Six packets cost one hundred and eighty.

95
00:03:35,922 --> 00:03:36,822
True.

96
00:03:37,151 --> 00:03:38,051
Step four.

97
00:03:37,916 --> 00:03:40,218
Seven packets cost two hundred and ten.

98
00:03:40,218 --> 00:03:41,118
False.

99
00:03:41,504 --> 00:03:42,921
So x is one to six.

100
00:03:42,921 --> 00:03:45,173
The most he can buy is six packets.

101
00:03:45,822 --> 00:03:47,287
Now, the exam trap.

102
00:03:47,637 --> 00:03:50,086
A question says: not
more than eight people.

103
00:03:50,086 --> 00:03:52,334
Many students write p is less than eight.

104
00:03:52,734 --> 00:03:55,059
But not more than eight lets eight in.

105
00:03:55,059 --> 00:03:57,349
So p is less than or equal to eight.

106
00:03:57,349 --> 00:04:01,265
At most, at least and not more
than always include the end number.

107
00:04:01,915 --> 00:04:03,439
Where is this asked?

108
00:04:03,439 --> 00:04:08,553
We found no past paper question on just
this first idea, so no chip lights.

109
00:04:08,553 --> 00:04:12,961
The type: turn words into an
inequality, then count its answers.

110
00:04:13,561 --> 00:04:14,637
Exam level now.

111
00:04:14,637 --> 00:04:17,629
How many integers make this
double inequality true?

112
00:04:17,629 --> 00:04:18,750
Pause and solve.

113
00:04:24,150 --> 00:04:25,703
The answer is B.

114
00:04:25,703 --> 00:04:26,603
Seven.

115
00:04:26,937 --> 00:04:29,237
Take away two from all three parts.

116
00:04:29,237 --> 00:04:33,361
Minus nine is at most three x,
and three x is less than twelve.

117
00:04:33,861 --> 00:04:36,189
Divide all three parts by three.

118
00:04:36,189 --> 00:04:38,833
Three is positive, so nothing flips.

119
00:04:38,833 --> 00:04:42,593
Minus three is at most x,
and x is less than four.

120
00:04:43,093 --> 00:04:45,262
Minus three is in, four is out.

121
00:04:45,262 --> 00:04:47,626
So x runs from minus three to three.

122
00:04:47,626 --> 00:04:48,673
Seven integers.

123
00:04:49,273 --> 00:04:50,814
Here is your recap.

124
00:04:50,814 --> 00:04:52,398
Screenshot this card.

125
00:04:55,598 --> 00:04:57,784
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

126
00:04:58,184 --> 00:04:59,084
One.

127
00:04:58,627 --> 00:05:02,560
What is the difference between less
than, and less than or equal to?

128
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:04,360
Two.

129
00:05:03,886 --> 00:05:06,639
Write spend at most fifty
rupees as an inequality.

130
00:05:07,539 --> 00:05:08,439
Three.

131
00:05:08,095 --> 00:05:11,761
Why does x plus two less than
five have endless real answers?

132
00:05:12,861 --> 00:05:16,837
Next time: multiply both sides by
minus one, and the arrow flips.

133
00:05:16,837 --> 00:05:17,737
Why?

134
00:05:17,296 --> 00:05:19,832
We find out in solving
linear inequalities.
